{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1846 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d95601e76ef2293adcd3732afea576daa8a70694409df452252c1b7ee441fb02" :edb/db-before-t 1845 :edb/db-after-t 1846 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535158 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:16:00.413Z" 13194139535158 true] [17592186166645 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/765415456d7f33c3558e08e7c4fc01ef3e80961c1e073a210033d91741d78f60.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the arc of a semicircle of radius $4$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." 13194139535158 true] [17592186166645 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s$, where $C$ is the arc of a semicircle of radius $4$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/238460fd6d89a120c21c88a681a7f2e3.png)" 13194139535158 false] [17592186166645 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C$, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L\n$$\n\nIn this case, $C$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 4$. Its arc length is given by\n\n$$\nL = πr = 4π\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 4\\pi\n$$" 13194139535158 false] [17592186166645 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $C$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 4.$ Its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=\\pi r=4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 4\\pi.\n$$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186166646 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/84de7754ed9244e3d646e452f6ed6dab349c130d17eae1d4dbc94cb52bd761e1.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above, traversed once counterclockwise." 13194139535158 true] [17592186166646 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s$, where $C$ is the path shown above, traversed once counterclockwise.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/bb0d714825f714ebc1e0612dd9fc507e.png)" 13194139535158 false] [17592186166646 1145 "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/f4e80a23910612bd55e7578a074f23c76535139889faff479c4dd0521e7496ff.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C = C_1 \\cup C_2 \\cup C_3$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{C_1}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_2}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_3}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nThe path $C_1$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 3.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} \\,\\textrm ds = \\dfrac{1}{2}(2 \\pi r) = 3\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe paths $C_2$ and $C_3$ are hypotenuses of right triangles whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} \\,\\textrm ds = \\int_{C_3} \\,\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 3\\pi + 5 + 5 = 3\\pi + 10.\n$$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186166646 1145 "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/2dab4e2a2bea7b9217543ff7144f9dce.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C = C_1 \\cup C_2 \\cup C_3$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n$\\int_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s = \\int_{C_1}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_2}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_3}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s$.\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C$, i.e.,\n$\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L$.\n\nThe path $C_1$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 3$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} \\,\\textrm ds = \\dfrac{1}{2}(2 \\pi r) = 3\\pi\n$$\n\nThe paths $C_2$ and $C_3$ are hypotenuses of right triangles whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} \\,\\textrm ds = \\int_{C_3} \\,\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 3\\pi + 5 + 5 = 3\\pi + 10\n$$" 13194139535158 false] [17592186166647 1100 "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2$, defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}C_{1}:r_{1}(t)|= ⟨\\frac{1}{9}t^{3}, t⟩, & 0 \\le t \\le 3 \\\\ C_{2}:r_{2}(u)|= ⟨3u^{3}, 3u⟩, & 0 \\le u \\le 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow suppose $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k$. Which of the following must be true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k$\n2. $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$" 13194139535158 false] [17592186166647 1100 "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{3},\\,t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨3{u}^{3},\\,3u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186166647 1145 "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Notice that $C_1$ and $C_2$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n$\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$.\nTo see that $C_1$ and $C_2$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n$x = \\dfrac{1}{9}y^3, \\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 3$.\n- Statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n$\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535158 false] [17592186166647 1145 "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Notice that $C_1$ and $C_2$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that $C_1$ and $C_2$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{9}y^3, \\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds  = \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535158 true] [17592186166792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334473 13194139535158 true] [17592186166792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334480 13194139535158 true] [17592186166792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334487 13194139535158 true] [17592186166792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334494 13194139535158 true] [17592186166793 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334522 13194139535158 true] [17592186166793 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334529 13194139535158 true] [17592186166793 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334536 13194139535158 true] [17592186166793 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334543 13194139535158 true] [17592186166794 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334501 13194139535158 true] [17592186166794 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334508 13194139535158 true] [17592186166794 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334515 13194139535158 true] [17592186166794 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334550 13194139535158 true] [17592186166794 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334557 13194139535158 true] [17592186167023 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535158 true] [17592186167023 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/558dbbc424725ca0d9bad628968489cc2e32d67136102290666ff055a634bc16.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is a path that traverses a semicircle of radius $2$ once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." 13194139535158 true] [17592186167023 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/q-122481.png)\n\nCalculate $∫_{C}ds$, where $C$ is a path that traverses a semicircle of radius $2$ once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." 13194139535158 false] [17592186167023 1145 "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/82fedadbb33b1747832bac25d9cda8e0b6b18b0fab411a870e90bc09ce47226e.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a semi-circle of radius $r=2.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\pi r=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is a line segment of length $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=2\\pi +4=2(\\pi +2).\n$$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186167027 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535158 true] [17592186167027 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/b7e72ce1ab38408f04f3b8d5a228ec0a3632c60086ef0a7abeb7e18af3002e6b.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." 13194139535158 true] [17592186167027 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3145/Source/Images/q-122768.png)\n\nCalculate $∫_{C}ds$, where $C$ is the line segment shown above." 13194139535158 false] [17592186167027 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=5.\n$$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334473 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334474 13194139535158 true] [17592186334473 1089 "q-122766" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334473 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535158 true] [17592186334473 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/1a46390ced6cc52c535f9f48e5e157cea83960caa339e98b23e3b64c93e0ea38.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." 13194139535158 true] [17592186334473 1113 #uuid "cf021e1e-faf4-5f6a-b0a8-f0391048effd" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334473 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is a line segment directed along the $y$-axis. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=3-(-1)=4.\n$$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334475 13194139535158 true] [17592186334474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334476 13194139535158 true] [17592186334474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334477 13194139535158 true] [17592186334474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334478 13194139535158 true] [17592186334474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334479 13194139535158 true] [17592186334474 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535158 true] [17592186334474 1041 "selection" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334474 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334478 13194139535158 true] [17592186334474 1110 #uuid "12b5156e-1434-5720-a491-1bfe4fd40d4e" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334475 1034 "3" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334475 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334475 1111 #uuid "0b3bf3d5-a4f3-57a2-8291-3c0f937003b1" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334476 1034 "2" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334476 1111 #uuid "1992564f-f46f-5ea5-a5ec-e09dea747ea2" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334477 1034 "6" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334477 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334477 1111 #uuid "195a7f3d-267c-5df0-ad28-1c8ce4d39285" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334478 1034 "4" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334478 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334478 1111 #uuid "accaba33-f571-51b6-a889-253355b3a8c2" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334479 1034 "5" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334479 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334479 1111 #uuid "3e956af7-df63-549e-98a7-24c79b7bc0bd" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334480 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334481 13194139535158 true] [17592186334480 1089 "q-122767" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334480 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535158 true] [17592186334480 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/d82f445e19af6e6d343bfab6c082108e945a8feb21caa9b60a2d93da24fc3f6a.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." 13194139535158 true] [17592186334480 1113 #uuid "ddead2b2-17bf-51e1-aac4-3b11ab66184f" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334480 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the line segment directed along the $x$-axis. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=5-1=4.\n$$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334482 13194139535158 true] [17592186334481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334483 13194139535158 true] [17592186334481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334484 13194139535158 true] [17592186334481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334485 13194139535158 true] [17592186334481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334486 13194139535158 true] [17592186334481 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535158 true] [17592186334481 1041 "selection" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334481 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334486 13194139535158 true] [17592186334481 1110 #uuid "dae23d7b-1c35-51a0-8ebb-0141af05e413" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334482 1034 "\\frac{7}{2}" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334482 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334482 1111 #uuid "579b8d90-5335-502b-a13d-775d474faa94" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334483 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334483 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334483 1111 #uuid "563d698c-dde5-5e01-8f5e-9d988ed701bc" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334484 1034 "3" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334484 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334484 1111 #uuid "fc20499b-2ad7-5309-b6ce-97c8fe704a44" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334485 1034 "2" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334485 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334485 1111 #uuid "c72de314-358c-5095-b195-5a29b2141ebc" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334486 1034 "4" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334486 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334486 1111 #uuid "afc6c891-b2de-5256-aa84-ed1822f7b011" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334487 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334488 13194139535158 true] [17592186334487 1089 "q-122769" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334487 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535158 true] [17592186334487 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/e41579712730d26c66d9f46f8d978fd51c8efbd76394c094113454e24b3aaff7.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." 13194139535158 true] [17592186334487 1113 #uuid "325c1c0b-4619-53ae-99d2-1cba6aa74c0f" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334487 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $2$ and $4:$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/849f44d06de55f14811d433f9ccfdd30d47f6a1db1970cdfbfbd6f16be4959b0.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334488 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334489 13194139535158 true] [17592186334488 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334490 13194139535158 true] [17592186334488 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334491 13194139535158 true] [17592186334488 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334492 13194139535158 true] [17592186334488 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334493 13194139535158 true] [17592186334488 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535158 true] [17592186334488 1041 "selection" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334488 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334489 13194139535158 true] [17592186334488 1110 #uuid "b699e459-3edc-5384-aa9f-ae389e93eb4b" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334489 1034 "2\\sqrt{5}" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334489 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334489 1111 #uuid "3a03023a-44c1-5e85-a695-d4df38e41cdb" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334490 1034 "2" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334490 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334490 1111 #uuid "eb6fed23-8f2c-5938-b6d6-f384473c7a6e" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334491 1034 "4" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334491 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334491 1111 #uuid "939d871c-b27f-5eb1-8568-428a6a66ec38" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334492 1034 "5" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334492 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334492 1111 #uuid "ac7515ce-8776-569a-b631-7ccc1e6ac79f" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334493 1034 "5\\sqrt{2}" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334493 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334493 1111 #uuid "fbb5f7a0-89a8-52b2-88ac-cff52778ed81" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334494 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334495 13194139535158 true] [17592186334494 1089 "q-122770" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334494 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535158 true] [17592186334494 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/69072873cb1f613ee9c2fa4a4caf3dc3409026c2a8310847b1c3dd890cc816d8.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." 13194139535158 true] [17592186334494 1113 #uuid "af85269c-7713-5875-ba80-2526a3b9960f" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334494 1145 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the path $C$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $3$ and $6:$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/d0d9f0fad038f04b6d93920979161694f4e709a31b68083f20e768bf60aa1675.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=3\\sqrt{5}.\n$$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334495 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334496 13194139535158 true] [17592186334495 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334497 13194139535158 true] [17592186334495 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334498 13194139535158 true] [17592186334495 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334499 13194139535158 true] [17592186334495 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334500 13194139535158 true] [17592186334495 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535158 true] [17592186334495 1041 "selection" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334495 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334497 13194139535158 true] [17592186334495 1110 #uuid "57df1786-42ba-5d4e-bd3c-a713cd14bfa1" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334496 1034 "3" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334496 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334496 1111 #uuid "123720a7-625b-54ef-afa7-ff47ae3cdf01" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334497 1034 "3\\sqrt{5}" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334497 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334497 1111 #uuid "33984fc4-9e03-5308-b670-89bec4b51ea9" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334498 1034 "5\\sqrt{3}" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334498 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334498 1111 #uuid "06f54f7d-7109-5dc8-8345-05ba8c05fa62" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334499 1034 "5" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334499 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334499 1111 #uuid "cd6ee277-bb02-564a-8958-406f8dbeb2b9" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334500 1034 "3\\sqrt{2}" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334500 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334500 1111 #uuid "7460fb06-39bc-5608-9475-a8a597e7de62" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334501 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334502 13194139535158 true] [17592186334501 1089 "q-122814" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334501 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535158 true] [17592186334501 1100 "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨{t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 2.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-4$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=8,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨16{u}^{4}-12{u}^{2},\\,2u⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334501 1113 #uuid "91efdf57-0580-5a7a-8027-92d6b70a8e32" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334501 1145 "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false, while statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx={y}^{4}-3{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334502 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334503 13194139535158 true] [17592186334502 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334504 13194139535158 true] [17592186334502 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334505 13194139535158 true] [17592186334502 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334506 13194139535158 true] [17592186334502 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334507 13194139535158 true] [17592186334502 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535158 true] [17592186334502 1041 "selection" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334502 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334505 13194139535158 true] [17592186334502 1110 #uuid "f85bffd2-e183-5912-8de1-0a4b30dc4f6d" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334503 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334503 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334503 1111 #uuid "bd4dd718-acd1-5774-ac4a-49915a8443da" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334504 1034 "I only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334504 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334504 1111 #uuid "548b7aff-51e9-5ba9-b465-ced3e7c97858" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334505 1034 "III only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334505 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334505 1111 #uuid "a5f57b57-688b-5c56-a764-79bd1b79cc5a" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334506 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334506 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334506 1111 #uuid "add131cc-01c7-5ecf-8c1e-8b7dccb11072" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334507 1034 "II only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334507 1111 #uuid "3852ac79-4729-51f8-805d-bfe694475237" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334508 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334509 13194139535158 true] [17592186334508 1089 "q-122815" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334508 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535158 true] [17592186334508 1100 "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(u)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{u}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{u}{2})⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 2\\pi .$ \n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=2,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le \\pi .$\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-1$\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334508 1113 #uuid "751a2612-5884-5a0e-995e-cbb5f375d2f8" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334508 1145 "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we note that both parameterizations traverse a half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ between $0$ and $\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334510 13194139535158 true] [17592186334509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334511 13194139535158 true] [17592186334509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334512 13194139535158 true] [17592186334509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334513 13194139535158 true] [17592186334509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334514 13194139535158 true] [17592186334509 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535158 true] [17592186334509 1041 "selection" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334509 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334512 13194139535158 true] [17592186334509 1110 #uuid "048cc43f-95ae-5086-a05f-0545a0f79471" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334510 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334510 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334510 1111 #uuid "87493a09-01cf-5e0f-abeb-6b6030e73069" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334511 1034 "I only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334511 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334511 1111 #uuid "a7c48ee8-1248-5d89-9da9-2d9249e1f9b6" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334512 1034 "III only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334512 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334512 1111 #uuid "9d50c880-12a2-5cc7-99ff-f6cbb365654c" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334513 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334513 1111 #uuid "1b46af28-d545-5444-a891-9d71de90793a" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334514 1034 "II only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334514 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334514 1111 #uuid "2ce3bb0a-e473-5af6-9bb8-1958785efac9" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334515 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334516 13194139535158 true] [17592186334515 1089 "q-122816" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334515 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535158 true] [17592186334515 1100 "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 4.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3$\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-3$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=12,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨4u,\\,8{u}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334515 1113 #uuid "a0ee2acf-324d-5412-b01f-2421ba3682e6" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334515 1145 "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3/2},\\,0\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334516 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334517 13194139535158 true] [17592186334516 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334518 13194139535158 true] [17592186334516 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334519 13194139535158 true] [17592186334516 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334520 13194139535158 true] [17592186334516 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334521 13194139535158 true] [17592186334516 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535158 true] [17592186334516 1041 "selection" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334516 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334519 13194139535158 true] [17592186334516 1110 #uuid "9f7cc522-1a3c-5573-b70a-f313e7a4fd67" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334517 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334517 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334517 1111 #uuid "0f13ff9f-d65a-511d-b0b9-1f82fb9dd65a" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334518 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334518 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334518 1111 #uuid "33201e7e-23e1-5f63-9641-06ff5682e032" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334519 1034 "I only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334519 1111 #uuid "1b184b9f-b217-556c-8b33-e5ae1142794a" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334520 1034 "III only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334520 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334520 1111 #uuid "1b07dc0a-5454-5714-a318-baaecae6710b" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334521 1034 "II only" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334521 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535158 true] [17592186334521 1111 #uuid "fd0da72a-0e55-585d-ac97-ef2412fc558c" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334522 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334523 13194139535158 true] [17592186334522 1089 "q-122828" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334522 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535158 true] [17592186334522 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/39a7cb0b31cb5d906803063597ae968be8fba90f048a4d9745225de7c3f8d6db.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." 13194139535158 true] [17592186334522 1113 #uuid "f4276705-6aa2-5565-947d-f686c87d0cd4" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334522 1145 "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/058c7b33112329df321e61660df4f2d388f42ca40c0aae5b73a089a0251726e2.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe paths ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are line segments of length $3.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=3+5+3=11.\n$$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334523 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334524 13194139535158 true] [17592186334523 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334525 13194139535158 true] [17592186334523 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334526 13194139535158 true] [17592186334523 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334527 13194139535158 true] [17592186334523 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334528 13194139535158 true] [17592186334523 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535158 true] [17592186334523 1041 "selection" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334523 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334526 13194139535158 true] [17592186334523 1110 #uuid "5850c0fc-03ca-537b-9cea-9ab26ce1f0d0" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334524 1034 "12" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334524 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334524 1111 #uuid "be9c52fe-d752-5698-bd06-b011753fc545" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334525 1034 "9" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334525 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334525 1111 #uuid "edd730ad-b3be-5096-a165-233ad0289287" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334526 1034 "11" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334526 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334526 1111 #uuid "01926086-4531-51f4-ac0f-6a59371b1169" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334527 1034 "10" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334527 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334527 1111 #uuid "5f73e647-e40d-5eec-9d33-4da67adacba0" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334528 1034 "8" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334528 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334528 1111 #uuid "b1290eb8-cefa-5a26-a0b