ure-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/27c19b56a24315f1105539afc441a33957dddf606b3410b86121e7cb8ed16973.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $2$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=5+2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334537 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334538 13194139535158 true] [17592186334537 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334539 13194139535158 true] [17592186334537 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334540 13194139535158 true] [17592186334537 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334541 13194139535158 true] [17592186334537 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334542 13194139535158 true] [17592186334537 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535158 true] [17592186334537 1041 "selection" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334537 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334539 13194139535158 true] [17592186334537 1110 #uuid "a71c9b66-3984-5ce2-84e9-16997a12ff54" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334538 1034 "2(3+\\sqrt{5})" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334538 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334538 1111 #uuid "e331d612-9168-5ac4-911d-eefdc7b69a28" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334539 1034 "5+2\\sqrt{5}" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334539 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334539 1111 #uuid "25cf667f-4eac-538b-8986-8d4c02956725" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334540 1034 "2(2+\\sqrt{5})" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334540 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334540 1111 #uuid "0cca7cee-927e-5e51-97ae-c2f9f11fda07" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334541 1034 "9" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334541 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334541 1111 #uuid "0d5bd321-845e-57b6-8d39-8d7efe1085f8" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334542 1034 "10" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334542 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334542 1111 #uuid "cb1b6ee4-41ca-5e93-8049-46c46004fdcc" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334543 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334544 13194139535158 true] [17592186334543 1089 "q-122850" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334543 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535158 true] [17592186334543 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/bf87c4707c0f4a8ab1b5157a13e8c7785da915833c5f9d896a2747af27f70e59.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." 13194139535158 true] [17592186334543 1113 #uuid "86bf6c2c-defc-5c47-a3d1-c7d99add5f71" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334543 1145 "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057099/assets/ff52b8e83a9cc9867b25ea3c24ed0f988a27dec73f9e0ab2dfda28ddabb3238a.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=2\\pi +4\\sqrt{2}=2(\\pi +2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334544 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334545 13194139535158 true] [17592186334544 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334546 13194139535158 true] [17592186334544 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334547 13194139535158 true] [17592186334544 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334548 13194139535158 true] [17592186334544 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334549 13194139535158 true] [17592186334544 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535158 true] [17592186334544 1041 "selection" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334544 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334545 13194139535158 true] [17592186334544 1110 #uuid "bbdc4de7-4d4c-520e-8524-9b0e3ae515ee" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334545 1034 "2(\\pi +2\\sqrt{2})" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334545 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334545 1111 #uuid "befb6801-2837-51de-9934-f8a0f4f8bee8" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334546 1034 "2(2\\pi +\\sqrt{2})" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334546 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334546 1111 #uuid "b8909c63-ceb4-5c55-8440-e72da28c2d88" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334547 1034 "\\frac{3\\pi +4\\sqrt{2}}{4}" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334547 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334547 1111 #uuid "2fedd427-c97f-5fa2-98b0-c2ca918ef2e0" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334548 1034 "2\\pi +3\\sqrt{2}" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334548 1111 #uuid "4c3e7afb-b7a5-52a8-96cf-477e9d3feff5" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334549 1034 "\\frac{\\pi +4\\sqrt{2}}{4}" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334549 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535158 true] [17592186334549 1111 #uuid "2fa6c612-f3e2-5622-a20d-d14547a6abcb" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334550 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334551 13194139535158 true] [17592186334550 1089 "q-122851" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334550 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535158 true] [17592186334550 1100 "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+1},\\,3t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+1},\\,2u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-k$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{2k}{3}$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334550 1113 #uuid "714b1c37-82c2-52dc-8892-8df8ef438e81" 13194139535158 true] [17592186334550 1145 "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},\\,0\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.